<UserControl x:Class="Bws.Gui.DetailsView"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             Focusable="False"
             IsTabStop="False"
             Grid.IsSharedSizeScope="True"
             Width="380">

    <!--
        EVERYTHING THIS WINDOW KNOWS ABOUT ONE ENTRY - the second half of `S7`, built 2026-08-13.

        A UserControl RATHER THAN A TEMPLATE IN THE THEME, AND THAT IS A DECISION ABOUT WHAT KIND OF
        THING THIS IS. The theme files hold the LOOK OF A CONTROL - a chip, a cell, a heading. This is
        a PART OF THE WINDOW, like the list and the row of filters, and a part of the window with its
        own file is what a UserControl is for. It also gets what a template cannot: a code-behind
        class, so the close button needs no lookup by name.

        It carries its own budget under the same markup ceiling as every other file here, which is
        the whole reason backlog 183 does not need answering - the panel asked for no room in
        Controls.xaml, because it declares no style of its own. Every face below is one the window
        already had.

        WIDTH IS A NUMBER WRITTEN HERE AND THAT IS THE ONE DEVIATION, SAID RATHER THAN HIDDEN.
        `ADR-23` wants appearance values named in Themes/Values.xaml, and that file stands EXACTLY on
        the markup ceiling, which may only ever go down - so there is nowhere to put a name today.
        `Width` is not among the attributes AppearanceGuards polices, and this window already carries
        its own size the same way for the same kind of reason, so no guard is being worked around.
        It moves into the theme the day backlog 173 gives Values.xaml room.

        Fixed rather than shared, because the content decides nothing useful: a description of 1251
        characters and a security descriptor of several hundred would make a panel as wide as the
        screen, and the list is what the window is for.

        Focusable and IsTabStop are off because a ContentControl - which is what a UserControl is -
        is BOTH BY DEFAULT, measured rather than assumed on 2026-08-13. Without them the panel is a
        tab stop that does nothing, in front of the close button that does. `docs/10` trap 8.
    -->

    <UserControl.Style>
        <Style TargetType="UserControl">
            <!--
                CLOSED UNTIL SOMEBODY ASKS, and it collapses rather than hiding so the column it
                sits in gives its width back to the list. The layout file of `S6d3` deliberately
                does not remember whether it was open - that would be a change to a schema frozen
                the day before, and the panel is one key press away.
            -->
            <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
            <Style.Triggers>
                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Showing}" Value="True">
                    <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Visible" />
                </DataTrigger>
            </Style.Triggers>
        </Style>
    </UserControl.Style>

    <UserControl.Resources>
        <!--
            ONE FIELD: WHAT IT IS CALLED, AND WHAT THIS ENTRY SAYS IN IT.

            The label column is shared across every section - Grid.IsSharedSizeScope above - so all
            five sections line up in one column of labels rather than five columns that nearly do.
            Auto rather than a number, so the widest heading decides and nothing is cut.

            THE FIXED WIDTH FACE IS A TRIGGER RATHER THAN A SECOND TEMPLATE. A launch path and a
            security descriptor are values where alignment carries meaning and two different things
            must not be able to look the same - `docs/03` part 4, and the list already draws them
            that way. Which fields those are is decided in the catalogue, not here.

            BasedOn ON THE IMPLICIT STYLE, WHICH IS TRAP 4 OF `docs/10` OBEYED. An explicit style
            REPLACES an implicit one rather than adding to it, so without this line these values
            would come out in the framework's own face inside a Fluent window.
        -->
        <DataTemplate x:Key="DetailLineTemplate">
            <Grid Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}">
                <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" SharedSizeGroup="DetailLabel" />
                    <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

                <TextBlock Grid.Column="0"
                           Text="{Binding Label}"
                           Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                           Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />

                <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                           Text="{Binding Value}"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap">
                    <TextBlock.Style>
                        <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource {x:Type TextBlock}}">
                            <Style.Triggers>
                                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding FixedWidth}" Value="True">
                                    <Setter Property="FontFamily" Value="{StaticResource TextFaceFixed}" />
                                </DataTrigger>
                            </Style.Triggers>
                        </Style>
                    </TextBlock.Style>
                </TextBlock>
            </Grid>
        </DataTemplate>

        <!--
            ONE HEADED GROUP OF FIELDS. The headings are the column picker's own groups, decided
            once in ViewModels/Columns.cs, so somebody who learned where a field lives while
            choosing columns finds it in the same place while reading about an entry.

            SECTIONS RATHER THAN TABS, and that is a deliberate departure from the word "tabbed" in
            section B of the specification. At five sections with nothing to edit, one scroll is
            read faster than tabs that hide four fifths of the answer. What will undo that is
            events, history and recovery arriving - content nobody reads in one pass.

            The note is empty on all but one of them, which is the ordinary case, so it takes no
            room when there is nothing to say.
        -->
        <DataTemplate x:Key="DetailSectionTemplate">
            <StackPanel Margin="{StaticResource MarginGroup}">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Heading}"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           ToolTip="{Binding Heading}"
                           Style="{StaticResource HeadingText}" />

                <TextBlock Text="{Binding Note}"
                           TextWrapping="Wrap"
                           Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}">
                    <TextBlock.Style>
                        <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource SubduedText}">
                            <Style.Triggers>
                                <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Note}" Value="">
                                    <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                                </DataTrigger>
                            </Style.Triggers>
                        </Style>
                    </TextBlock.Style>
                </TextBlock>

                <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Lines}"
                              ItemTemplate="{StaticResource DetailLineTemplate}" />
            </StackPanel>
        </DataTemplate>
    </UserControl.Resources>

    <Grid Margin="{StaticResource PadPanel}">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            <RowDefinition Height="*" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!--
            BOTH NAMES, AND NEITHER STANDS ALONE - `ADR-14`. The display name reads as the heading
            because it is the one somebody recognises, and the internal name is under it because it
            is the one they will type into a command. Each gives way with an ellipsis and carries
            the whole of itself in a tooltip, exactly as every cell in the list does.
        -->
        <Grid Grid.Row="0">
            <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
                <ColumnDefinition Width="*" />
                <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

            <StackPanel Grid.Column="0">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding ShownLabel}"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           ToolTip="{Binding ShownLabel}"
                           Style="{StaticResource HeadingText}" />

                <TextBlock Text="{Binding ShownName}"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           ToolTip="{Binding ShownName}"
                           Style="{StaticResource SubduedText}" />
            </StackPanel>

            <!--
                A VISIBLE WAY OUT, not only a key. Escape closes this and the tooltip says so, but
                a panel whose only exit is a key nobody was told about is the third of Nielsen's
                heuristics broken - `docs/11` 9.2 names it as this product's weakest. It is also
                the only thing in this panel the keyboard stops on, which is why the panel itself
                is not a tab stop.
            -->
            <Button Grid.Column="1"
                    AutomationProperties.AutomationId="detailsClose"
                    Content="{DynamicResource gui.details.close}"
                    ToolTip="{DynamicResource gui.details.close.hint}"
                    Margin="{StaticResource MarginBetweenControls}"
                    VerticalAlignment="Top"
                    Click="CloseRequested" />
        </Grid>

        <!--
            THE ENTRY LEFT THE MACHINE WHILE THIS WAS OPEN, and rule 8 is why this line exists: a
            panel that carried on showing the last reading would answer questions about a service
            that is not there. It says the values below are the last thing read rather than blanking
            them, because what was read is still the most useful thing anybody has.
        -->
        <TextBlock Grid.Row="1"
                   AutomationProperties.AutomationId="detailsNotice"
                   Text="{Binding Notice}"
                   TextWrapping="Wrap"
                   Margin="{StaticResource MarginAboveStatus}">
            <TextBlock.Style>
                <Style TargetType="TextBlock" BasedOn="{StaticResource NoticeText}">
                    <Style.Triggers>
                        <DataTrigger Binding="{Binding Notice}" Value="">
                            <Setter Property="Visibility" Value="Collapsed" />
                        </DataTrigger>
                    </Style.Triggers>
                </Style>
            </TextBlock.Style>
        </TextBlock>

        <!--
            Scrolls vertically and never sideways. A value too long for the width wraps, because
            this panel is the place a person comes to READ a description of 1251 characters - the
            cell it came from is the place that trims.
        -->
        <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="2"
                      VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                      HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
            <ItemsControl AutomationProperties.AutomationId="detailSections"
                          ItemsSource="{Binding Sections}"
                          ItemTemplate="{StaticResource DetailSectionTemplate}" />
        </ScrollViewer>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
